    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d931">

                <pb facs="0866" n="762" sameAs="frus:frus1913/pg762"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.00/6631.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Ambassador to
                        the Secretary of State.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1913-03-11T12:00:00-06:36">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 11, 1913—noon</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>98. Carranza, defeated near Saltillo, has retreated to Monclova, where his forces
                    will in all probability be dispersed. This will leave <pb facs="0867" n="763"
                        xml:id="pg763"/> the revolutionary movement in the north terminated except
                    in the State of Sonora. The attention of the Department should be sharply
                    directed to Sonora and to Lower California, or international complications may
                    be brought about by intrigues on both sides of the frontier.</p>
                <p>If any of our consuls have inadvertently been sympathizing with local activities
                    against the Federal Government for supposedly good motives they should, in my
                    opinion, be instructed to avoid such complications and to work for submission to
                    the Federal authorities, by which alone peace can be restored.</p>
                <p>The rest of Mexico, so far as the advices of the Embassy are concerned, is at
                    peace except for a revolt of rurales in the State of Guerrero, which I do not
                    consider formidable.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Henry Lane Wilson</hi>.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
